1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,593
Book Four, Proposition Thirteen.

2
00:00:02,593 --> 00:00:08,026
In a given pentagon, which is equilateral and equiangular, to inscribe a circle.

3
00:00:08,026 --> 00:00:16,650
Let A B C D E be the given equilateral and equiangular pentagon; it is required to inscribe a circle in the pentagon A B C D E.

4
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:29,238
Bisect the adjacent angles B C D and C D E by the straight lines C F and D F, and from the point F where they meet, join F B, F A, and F E.

5
00:00:29,238 --> 00:00:38,080
From F, draw F G, F H, F K, F L, and F M perpendicular to the five sides A B, B C, C D, D E, and E A.

6
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:51,304
Because B C equals C D, C F is common, and angle B C F equals angle D C F, by One Four triangles B C F and D C F are congruent, so angle C B F equals angle C D F.

7
00:00:51,304 --> 00:01:04,504
Since angle A B C equals angle C D E, which is double angle C D F, angle A B C is also double angle C B F; thus F B—and similarly F A and F E—bisects its vertex angle.

8
00:01:04,504 --> 00:01:15,491
Now in right triangles F H C and F K C, the angles at C are equal, the right angles at H and K are equal, and the hypotenuse F C is common.

9
00:01:15,491 --> 00:01:25,212
By One Twenty-Six, F H equals F K, and by the same reasoning all five perpendiculars F G, F H, F K, F L, and F M are equal.

10
00:01:25,212 --> 00:01:37,385
Therefore the circle described with center F and radius F G passes through G, H, K, L, and M, and by Three Sixteen touches all five sides at right angles.

11
00:01:37,385 --> 00:01:42,680
Thus circle G H K L M has been inscribed in the pentagon A B C D E.

12
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:44,340
Which was to be done.

13
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:51,318
Proposition Four Thirteen generalizes the triangle incenter construction of Four Four to the regular pentagon.

14
00:01:51,318 --> 00:02:03,598
As Augustus De Morgan observed, Euclid's proof works for any regular polygon: bisecting two adjacent angles determines the symmetry center F, and dropping perpendicular apothems yields the inscribed circle.

15
00:02:03,598 --> 00:02:15,760
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixty-five combines Side-Angle-Side and Angle-Angle-Side triangle congruence with the tangent criterion of Three Sixteen, building on forty-five ancestor nodes.

